                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "03e3219f-cf8d-4820-a317-7e7ed1974016",
                        "entry_id": "0437d66d-5e36-32a5-9fbd-ce690859c209",
                        "tags": [
                            "participle",
                            "past"
                        ],
                        "form": "sermonized"
                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "d59dc6f8-8810-4c93-8c12-7ec27f13cae4",
                        "entry_id": "0437d66d-5e36-32a5-9fbd-ce690859c209",
                        "tags": [
                            "past"
                        ],
                        "form": "sermonized"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "1db4fdc2-354c-38a1-9aa7-f8ad923118d8",
                        "entry_id": "0437d66d-5e36-32a5-9fbd-ce690859c209",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To speak in the manner of a sermon; to preach; to propagate one's morality or opinions with speech."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "intransitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "9bda4a66-7f1f-46ed-81da-c62f5a4f6c5a",
                                "sense_id": "1db4fdc2-354c-38a1-9aa7-f8ad923118d8",
                                "text": "And doe not our Prelates thus, when […] they disgrace and traduce Preaching, calling it in scorne, Sermonizing?"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "fa6c244e-3b62-4a99-acdd-905375982549",
                                "sense_id": "1db4fdc2-354c-38a1-9aa7-f8ad923118d8",
                                "text": "Don’t be alarmed, I am not going to sermonize—but what is almost as dull, to narrate."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "fc48555b-595c-435c-9394-14731dd875de",
                                "sense_id": "1db4fdc2-354c-38a1-9aa7-f8ad923118d8",
                                "text": "“[…] I am perhaps talking rather superfluously; but a man likes to assume superiority over himself, by holding up his bad example and sermonizing on it.”"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "27013bf4-ec58-4ff5-a7bc-679b3935faa3",
                                "sense_id": "1db4fdc2-354c-38a1-9aa7-f8ad923118d8",
                                "text": "[…] she would distrust him if she saw him again before he had achieved all his promises; had journeyed clear out to the Mississippi […] and had become fluent in sermonizing in the Dakota language. No, he must see her next in his glory as a practicing missionary."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4a23474f-64cd-479b-8bd0-39bb499a955d",
                                "sense_id": "1db4fdc2-354c-38a1-9aa7-f8ad923118d8",
                                "text": "Sometimes Claude would lead Joseph to the Louvre—a magnificence Joseph had never before entered—and point out the darker paintings and sermonize on them; Claude was an aesthete."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "246957b3-9be8-310a-8775-118d3defe965",
                        "entry_id": "0437d66d-5e36-32a5-9fbd-ce690859c209",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To preach a sermon to (somebody); to give (somebody) instruction or admonishment on the basis of one's morality or opinions."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "ae066db7-a00c-4f78-af23-8f0b5b785da2",
                                "sense_id": "246957b3-9be8-310a-8775-118d3defe965",
                                "text": "He wishes, I suppose, to sermonize me: but I shall not give him an opportunity—"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "140cc0e7-999b-499b-b5f3-ce40a87d0ffd",
                                "sense_id": "246957b3-9be8-310a-8775-118d3defe965",
                                "text": "“[…] we sermonised her on the presumption of attempting to teach such clever blades as we were, when she was herself so ignorant.”"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "97dde699-04f3-4162-898c-63c4c2d1c15b",
                                "sense_id": "246957b3-9be8-310a-8775-118d3defe965",
                                "text": "Why was the choice o’ the man to niche himself\nPerversely ’neath the tower where Time’s own tongue\nThus undertakes to sermonize the world?"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8a5f4384-3305-4cdc-b8f9-ffe6c35b63ca",
                                "sense_id": "246957b3-9be8-310a-8775-118d3defe965",
                                "text": "He could talk morals to others in the grand manner, and with positive enjoyment, but to be sermonized himself secretly exasperated him because it constrained him and made him self-conscious."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "52ecbc57-0d8e-4f51-87ec-4a8be733778d",
                                "sense_id": "246957b3-9be8-310a-8775-118d3defe965",
                                "text": "[…] maybe she sermonized us both about love because of her sons."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "a6030edd-805c-34dc-943e-11f4ae691c39",
                        "entry_id": "0437d66d-5e36-32a5-9fbd-ce690859c209",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To say in the manner of a sermon or lecture."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "7e3132c3-e170-4592-8b99-825fe4c6b075",
                                "sense_id": "a6030edd-805c-34dc-943e-11f4ae691c39",
                                "text": "“Children, children! Woe, how you do sin!” Mrs. Kavarsky sermonized. “Come now, obey an older person […]”"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9373c7d9-ab2c-4500-8ae4-45f8583e83d5",
                                "sense_id": "a6030edd-805c-34dc-943e-11f4ae691c39",
                                "text": "1917, George Creel (uncredited author) and Douglas Fairbanks, Laugh and Live, New York: Britton, Chapter 17, p. 144,\nThen as one man they jumped to their feet and by reason of prolonged cheering gave national impulse to a thought which has since been sermonized from thousands of pulpits."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "828da6a1-c3ed-43ad-8f41-01a4787f7f6a",
                                "sense_id": "a6030edd-805c-34dc-943e-11f4ae691c39",
                                "text": "“I tell you, we’re in for bad times,” de Selva sermonized before a group. “Our haciendas are threatened by renegades […]”"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "22d8efff-0738-30b3-a733-c33126871a11",
                        "entry_id": "0437d66d-5e36-32a5-9fbd-ce690859c209",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To inculcate rigid rules."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "intransitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "1ebc16a5-2b1c-4867-9e99-606dbad40cb0",
                                "sense_id": "22d8efff-0738-30b3-a733-c33126871a11",
                                "text": "If you consider my letters in their true light as conveying to you the advice of a Friend, who sincerely wishes you happiness, and desires to promote your pleasures, you will both read and attend them; but, if you consider them in their opposite, and very false light, as the dictates of a morose and sermonizing Father, I am sure they will be not only unattended to, but unread."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
